rado.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Paul D. Bloom | CEO, President & Director | Age 51 | Compensation: $1.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 151 people. Revenue per employee of $1.1M ranks in the top quartile among Basic Materials peers, improving at 7%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Stock-based compensation of $61K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Gevo, Inc. is a small-cap basic materials business with a $344M market cap on $161M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +415% over the past three years and +18% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at -22% against a 12% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and -36% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The company is burning through $44M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 16.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin -1.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 4.5% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (-0.6) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Operating margins 22% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage 